{
  "comments": [
    {
      "number": 1,
      "title": "Qualify sign preservation as an estimand result",
      "quote": "I distinguish estimating absolute advisor accuracy from deciding whether the advisor is more accurate than oneself. Uniformly sampled disagreements preserve the sign of the paired accuracy difference, although comparing the advisor's selected accuracy with one's marginal accuracy can favour the inferior source.",
      "feedback": "I initially read “preserve the sign” as applying to the observed feedback sample, which matters for the finite-budget decision studied here. Let p be the proportion of disagreements on which the advisor is correct. The population accuracy difference is P(disagreement) × (2p − 1), and the expected paired difference under uniform disagreement sampling is 2p − 1, so their signs agree. A finite sample can still reverse the sign: if p = 0.6 and one disagreement is verified, the observed paired difference is −1 with probability 0.4. This is a clarity problem rather than an error in the population result. Rewrite “Uniformly sampled disagreements preserve the sign of the paired accuracy difference” as “Uniform sampling of disagreements preserves the sign of the expected paired accuracy difference” because individual feedback samples can point in the opposite direction.",
      "status": "Pending",
      "severity": "minor",
      "confidence": "high"
    }
  ]
}

